**Proposition 7.2 (exact conditional fragment account).** Use unit
charges for a fine commitment, endpoint probe, designated endpoint
assignment and created vertex. For one actual completed row fragment
let $B$ count unary SetBit reactions including no-ops/head marks;
$V$ one-vertex Births; $R$ reciprocal retargets; $A$ first activations;
and $E$ explicit assignments of an already empty old slot, each with
its own stage. Each reaction also relocates the controller with three
designated assignments. A Birth initializes seven new slots and the
one parent attachment. Retarget and activation use three and two
data assignments respectively. Let $O$ be 1 for an original OUT row
and 0 otherwise. For the unfused stage convention and its fused
version respectively,

$$
\begin{aligned}
N&=2+B+V+R+A+E+O,\\
N^*&=2+B+V+R+A+E,\\
g&=4N+P+3B+9V+3R+2A+E,\\
g^*&=4N^*+P^*+3B+9V+3R+2A+E.
\end{aligned}
\tag{OR.4}
$$

Here $P,P^*$ are the complete ordered sums of actual guard, control,
operand, matching, vacancy, reciprocity and return probes. Any further
primitive evaluation tariff must be added explicitly. Under the same
full-scaffold scan prescription, $P=6MN+P_{\rm data}$. If the deleted
pure Finish has no noncontrol probes and all retained probe lists
are unchanged, then

$$
g-g^*=(6M+4)O.
\tag{OR.5}
$$

Proof. Each reaction contributes one commitment and three controller
assignments, giving $4N$. Data assignments are
$3B+8V+3R+2A+E$; vertex charges add $V$. This proves (OR.4), including
unchanged assignments. The controller subtree contains $2M$ vertices
including its marker. Probing two child slots per vertex, a parent
return per nonroot vertex, and its entrance costs
$4M+(2M-1)+1=6M$ per specified complete scan. Fusion deletes one
such scan, one commitment and three controller assignments, while
keeping data effects and births identical. This gives (OR.5).
Other routing or evaluator changes instead contribute their actual
price difference. ∎

The $E$ coordinate makes the tariff convention explicit. UAS 2.2's
fresh finite bit designates both old socket child slots, including
the unchanged vacant one. A bare eight-assignment Birth designates
only the occupied attachment. To retain that literal UAS no-op
assignment in a fine schedule, add its actual guarded empty-slot
assignment and stage, or declare another primitive that includes it.
It cannot disappear from a purported identical assignment account.
For a row with $e$ core-plus-receipt cell appends and $s$ fresh finite
bits, $V=24e+s$ under the supplied one-vertex templates; this is reused
local material accounting, not a new constructor theorem. It says
nothing about the installed availability of those stages.

